"""
Public submission artefact for the Discovery-Bench task:
'In what context does the gardening pathway for introducing non-native
plants show different prevalence styles across habitat types?'

Wraps outputs of main_analysis.py and prints the data-grounded
natural-language hypothesis.
"""
import os, pandas as pd

ROOT   = "/workspace"
TBL    = os.path.join(ROOT, "artifacts", "tables")
SCRIPT = os.path.join(ROOT, "artifacts", "main_analysis.py")

def line(s="", ch="-"):
    print(ch * 70)
    print(s)
    print(ch * 70)

line("Workflow")
print("1. Inspect 4 input files: temporal_trends_contingency_table.csv,"
      " phylogenetic_tree.txt, invasion_success_pathways.csv,"
      " invaded_niche_pathways.csv.")
print("2. Build 3 prevalence metrics by habitat on the invaded subset (n=191):")
print("   - composition    : n.gard / (n.gard+n.unint+n.agfo)")
print("   - presence freq  : share of plots with any_gard>0  (Wilson 95% CI)")
print("   - abundance share: n.gard per plot (raw counts, sampling caveat).")
print("3. PCA on 24 landscape variables (18 coverage × year × buffer +"
      " climate + elevation + distances) to control multicollinearity.")
print("4. Sensitivity: repeat buffer-scale descriptors at 50/500/1000 m.")
print("5. Binomial GLM (any_gard) + logit-proportion OLS (HC0 SE,"
      " weights=n.total) over 191 invaded plots with habitat dummies"
      " (Urban = baseline), PCs, climate, distances and historical"
      " landscape-change dummies.")
print("6. Moran's I on deviance residuals (k=8 nearest-neighbour weights,"
      " 499 permutations); refit with degree-2 lon/lat trend if needed.")
print("7. Bridge to species file (invasion_success_pathways.csv): trait"
      " profile of the 42 'Gardening' species and its qualitative"
      " divergence from Unintentional weeds.")

line("Artefacts produced")
for fn in sorted(os.listdir(TBL)):
    full = os.path.join(TBL, fn)
    print(f"  tables/{fn}  ({os.path.getsize(full)} bytes)")
print(f"  main_analysis.py  ({os.path.getsize(SCRIPT)} bytes)")

line("Top-line metric table (Gardening prevalence by habitat)")
mt = pd.read_csv(os.path.join(TBL, "metrics_by_habitat.csv"))
print(mt[["habitat","n_plots_w_presence","comp_gard_over_g_u_a",
         "pres_anygard_freq","pres_ci_lo","pres_ci_hi",
         "abund_share_gard_per_plot","style_dom_pathway"]]
      .round(3).to_string(index=False))

line("Binomial GLM (any_gard) habitat contrasts vs Urban")
glm = pd.read_csv(os.path.join(TBL, "glm_anygard_binomial.csv"), index_col=0)
hab = glm.filter(like="hab_", axis=0).round(3)
print(hab.to_string())

line("Logit-proportion OLS (HC0 SE) significant predictors p<0.1")
lp = pd.read_csv(os.path.join(TBL, "glm_logitprop_robust.csv"), index_col=0)
print(lp[lp["pvalue"]<0.1].round(3).to_string())

line("Hypothesis (natural-language, data-grounded)")

print("""
The 'Gardening' pathway for non-native plant introductions in Barcelona
province shows three clearly distinct prevalence styles across habitat
types, and those styles track the joint ecological–anthropogenic context
around each plot:

  STYLE 1  ``Gardening-dominated coastal / wetland habitats''
            Coastal and Wetlands host the highest proportional load of
            Gardening species (~0.5–0.6 of all introductions recorded at
            those plots) and the highest presence rates (0.81–0.91 with
            tight Wilson 95% CIs), even though their absolute human-
            pressure profile (urban and cropland covers) is moderate.
            This signals that Gardening species are over-represented in
            humid, low-elevation but low-intensity agricultural habitats
            where ornamental horticulture propagates from gardens into
            near-natural landscape elements (riparian margins, dunes,
            coastal scrub).

  STYLE 2  ``Gardening-on-stable-historical-landscape plots''
            Holding habitat constant, a binomial GLM on any_gard
            (n=191) shows that the proportion of urban-vs-cropland
            change between 1956 and 2009 (progressive or no-change
            dummies vs the regressive reference) is the strongest
            non-habitat predictor of Gardening presence (OR 2.7–2.9,
            p<0.02). Once both habitat dummies and the historical-
            change variables enter the model, the proportion
            n.gard/(n.gard+n.unint+n.agfo) is also positively associated
            with PC1 (urbanisation gradient, p≈0.06) and PC2
            (agricultural intensity, p≈0.10), indicating that within
            plots that already contain non-natives, the share of
            Gardening species is highest in settled, stable landscape
            matrices.

  STYLE 3  ``Gardening-suppressed in Croplands and other intensively
            human-managed habitats''
            Croplands is the only habitat where the GLM-attributed odds
            of any_gard are significantly lower than the urban baseline
            (OR 0.19, p=0.011), and its raw composition (0.185) and
            presence (0.37) confirm Croplands as Unintentional-dominant.
            This matches the species-level bridge: the 42 Gardening
            species in the dataset have a markedly more specialist trait
            profile (lower habitat.range [1.95 vs 2.77], lower invaded
            niche breadth [0.46 vs 0.75], lower mean residence time
            [86 vs 104 yr], and lower per-species plot occupancy [3.9 vs
            9.6]) than the 30 Unintentional species, so they are not the
            weeds that dominate intensively disturbed cropland.

Caveats and limitations
  • 441/632 plots contain zero non-native species (n.total=0); 191 plots
    carry the entire signal. Within those, 5 habitats (Broad-leaved
    forests, Coniferous forests, Rock outcrops, Scrublands, Meadows)
    have 1–5 invaded plots each, so their extremal Style 1 values are
    exploratory, not confirmatory, and are flagged with Wilson CIs.
  • Moran's I on deviance residuals of the binomial GLM was borderline
    (I=0.067, perm-p=0.026); a degree-2 lon/lat trend refit brought it
    to p=0.258 and did not change the sign or rank of the habitat
    contrasts – conclusions are robust to spatial autocorrelation.
  • Patterns are reproduced qualitatively at the 50, 500 and 1000 m
    buffer scales (Wilson's-CI per habitat × buffer table available),
    although the 50-m descriptors are noisier because within-plot cover
    is dominated by zeros.
  • Phylogenetic triangulation was skipped (declared as optional and
    not informative at plot level: 81 tips = 81 species and the signal
    is at the plot–habitat stratum, not per species).
  • Causal interpretation of the historical-change coefficients requires
    caution; the coefficients are best read as associations conditional
    on habitat, bioclimatic gradient and landscape-disturbance proxies.
""")
